  "format": "news",
  "script": "WhatsApp is building a chat that WhatsApp itself says is not end-to-end encrypted.\n\nThose are WhatsApp's words, not mine. WABetaInfo found them in the Android beta.\n\nStrange thing for the encryption app to build. It makes more sense once you know what WhatsApp threw off the platform first.\n\nIt starts with a new Agents section in settings. You generate an API key and paste it into whatever's running the AI. Up to five. You name each one and give it a photo.\n\nSo it sits in your chat list looking like everybody else.\n\nBut that chat runs through a Meta service on the developer's behalf, so the developer can read what you send.\n\nThe fence is real, though. The agent sees its own chat. Not your contacts, not your media, not one other conversation. Your personal chats stay encrypted exactly as before.\n\nNow, what WhatsApp threw off the platform. Last October it barred general-purpose chatbots. By January, ChatGPT and Perplexity had stopped working there, and the EU, Italy and Brazil opened antitrust probes.\n\nEight months later, there's a door back in. Built by Meta, on Meta's servers.\n\nNobody has said those two are connected. It's still a handful of testers in a few countries. But if it ships, the only thing you need to know is which chat is which.",
